<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Moghulistan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Moghulistan was the eastern successor state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Empire</a>, based on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ili-river">Ili River</a> steppe. After the Timurid conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> in the fourteenth century, it remained the surviving Chagataid realm. It survived in diminished form for another century, often dominated by tribal leaders.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moghulistan.jpg" alt="Moghulistan" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ro4444 at English Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chagatai_Khanate_(1490).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Moghulistan, also called the Moghul Khanate or the Eastern Chagatai Khanate, was a Turco-Mongol, Muslim khanate that broke away from the Chagatai Khanate. The name also describes a geographic area north of the Tengri Tagh range, now parts of Kazakhstan, Kyrgyzstan and north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>. In the 1340s the eastern tribes, who had earlier revolted against a khan who moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a>, were virtually independent. In 1347 the most powerful of them, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dughlat">Dughlats</a>, raised the Chagatayid Tughlugh Timur to the throne as head of a tribal confederacy governing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a> and the steppe of Moghulistan.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughlugh-timur">Tughlugh Timur</a> converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in 1354 with some 160,000 followers, which marked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamization">Islamization</a> of Moghulistan. In 1360 and 1361 he invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> and installed his son Ilyas Khoja, but could not hold it, and the Moghuls were expelled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir-husayn">Amir Husayn</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Dughlat Amir Qamar-ud-Din likely killed Ilyas Khoja in 1368, which set off near-constant civil wars, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> sent at least five victorious expeditions against him. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a> opened caravan trade, and the khans grew rich collecting tax on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>. The Dughlats enthroned Khizr Khoja in the 1380s, and he forcibly converted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turfan</a> and ended their polity. Uwais Khan restored Moghul rule from 1418, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunus-khan">Yunus Khan</a>, who reigned from 1462, reunited the khanate and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurids</a>.</p><p>After Yunus died in 1487 his sons divided his lands, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-alaq">Ahmad Alaq</a> ruling the east from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turpan</a> and Mahmud Khan the west from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-shaybani">Muhammad Shaybani</a> defeated both brothers in 1503 and later executed Mahmud, and Kyrgyz tribes gradually took the western lands. In 1514 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-said-khan">Sultan Said Khan</a> conquered the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a> and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yarkent-khanate">Yarkent Khanate</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a>, so the khanate was permanently divided. Power then shifted to the khojas, Naqshbandi Sufi leaders, and by the early 18th century the Dzungar Khanate had conquered the region.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Unlike <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> elsewhere, those of Moghulistan were steppe nomads from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>, who kept their way of life for centuries and were among the last Mongols to convert to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. They adopted Turkic, forming the Chagatai Turkic language. Chieftains used Turkic and Turko-Persian titles such as Mirza, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bek">Bek</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">Amir</a>, and the khans later adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">sharia</a> in place of the Mongol Yassa. Local control rested with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dughlat">Dughlat</a> amirs or Naqshbandi Sufis in their oases, and the towns declined under nomadic rule. Most knowledge of the state comes from the Tarikh-i-Rashidi, the only primary source.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Moghulistan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Only <b>Moghulistan</b>, the eastern rump based on the Ili valley steppe and the Tarim Basin, was left as a surviving Chagataid state.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chagatai-empire">Chagatai Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…the rival line of Urus Khan, Kerei and his kinsman Janibek, broke with him, and around 1465, the date given by the historian Muhammad Haidar Dughlat, they led their followers south-east into <b>Moghulistan</b>, where Esen Buqa Khan allowed them to settle in the western Chu and Talas valleys.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kazakh-khanate">Kazakh Khanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1487 Yunus Khan's son Sultan Ahmad Khan ruled it, and Turpan became the capital of what is sometimes called Eastern <b>Moghulistan</b> or Uyghurstan.</blockquote><a href="/state/turpan-khanate">Turpan Khanate</a></li></ul></section>
